---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Status from '../components/Status.astro';
import { data, fmtDate } from '../lib/project';

const findings = [...data.findings].sort((a, b) => (a.date < b.date ? 1 : a.date > b.date ? -1 : 0));
const stageName = (id: string) => data.stages.find((s) => s.id === id)?.name ?? '';
---

<Base title="What we have learned — Minimum Viable Mind" description="Registered results, diagnostics ruled for the record, and the process lessons that changed how the work runs.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Learned</p>
    <h1>What the instruments have said so far</h1>
    <p class="lede">Registered results and diagnostics ruled for the record, newest first, each with what it found and what it changed. Nulls are findings here; the project is built so that every outcome is worth having.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="summary-row">
      <div><div class="stat">{data.derived.counts.findings}</div><div class="stat-label">findings on record</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat">{data.derived.counts.findings_by_kind.registered ?? 0}</div><div class="stat-label">registered results</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat">{data.derived.counts.findings_by_kind.process ?? 0}</div><div class="stat-label">process lessons</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    {findings.map((f) => (
      <article class="finding" id={f.id}>
        <header class="f-head">
          <span class="mono">{fmtDate(f.date)} · Stage {f.stage}, {stageName(f.stage)}</span>
          <Status value={f.kind} />
        </header>
        <h2 class="f-title">{f.title}</h2>
        <p class="prose">{f.what}</p>
        <p class="f-sowhat"><span class="kicker-inline">So what</span> {f.so_what}</p>
        <p class="f-source mono">{f.source}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="callout">
      <p class="kicker" style="margin-top:0">The arc in one paragraph</p>
      <p class="prose" style="margin:0">Instruments that can lose exist and they won: the first registered removal test found a router, not a centre, and the self-report was never subtracted. Alignment training moves the policy, not the geometry. Under pressure, frontier models mask rather than capitulate. An installed self-register in a purpose-built model turned out to be a mural, so the centre has to be acquired; an acquired one is learnable, ownership-specific and replicated, and not yet localised. What the paper can claim is a structural signature of self-indexing in small constructed models. Never a conscious machine.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
